{"claim_id":"clm_opt_matrix_shampoo_1","claim_type":"method_definition","extracted_at":"2026-07-19","fingerprint":"0a1111bf70842460","limitations":["approximation to full-matrix preconditioning, not exact","assumes benefit from per-axis structure"],"locator":"method","paper_id":"pap_opt_matrix_shampoo","scope":{"algorithm":"shampoo","parameter_structure":"matrix_or_tensor"},"statement":"Shampoo maintains one full preconditioning matrix per dimension (mode) of a parameter tensor and applies each by contracting over the remaining dimensions, yielding a structure-aware approximation to full-matrix AdaGrad.","tags":["shampoo","kronecker_factorization","preconditioner","full_matrix_adagrad"]}
{"claim_id":"clm_opt_matrix_shampoo_2","claim_type":"descriptive","extracted_at":"2026-07-19","fingerprint":"be1caa36f904d1ed","limitations":["still quadratic in each individual dimension size","cost grows with wide layers"],"locator":"method","paper_id":"pap_opt_matrix_shampoo","scope":{"algorithm":"shampoo","cost_axis":"memory_and_compute"},"statement":"Per-axis preconditioning cuts preconditioner memory/compute from the O((prod d_i)^2) of full-matrix methods to O(sum d_i^2), making matrix preconditioning tractable for large layers.","tags":["scalability","memory","computational_cost"]}
{"claim_id":"clm_opt_matrix_shampoo_3","claim_type":"predictive","extracted_at":"2026-07-19","fingerprint":"7a0c910a4923512e","limitations":["per-step wall-clock cost is higher than first-order methods","original evaluation predates large transformer LMs"],"locator":"results","paper_id":"pap_opt_matrix_shampoo","scope":{"metric":"steps_to_target","models":"deep_networks"},"statement":"On deep-learning models Shampoo reaches target loss in substantially fewer optimization steps than commonly used first-order optimizers (SGD/AdaGrad/Adam).","tags":["convergence_speed","sample_efficiency","second_order"]}
{"claim_id":"clm_opt_matrix_scalable_second_order_1","claim_type":"method_definition","extracted_at":"2026-07-19","fingerprint":"489076f5c9f0cd95","limitations":["requires heterogeneous CPU+accelerator infrastructure","adds engineering complexity"],"locator":"method","paper_id":"pap_opt_matrix_scalable_second_order","scope":{"algorithm":"distributed_shampoo","hardware":"accelerator_plus_cpu"},"statement":"Distributed Shampoo offloads the expensive inverse p-th-root preconditioner computation to host CPUs and runs it asynchronously, overlapping preconditioner construction with accelerator training to hide its cubic cost.","tags":["distributed_shampoo","preconditioner_computation","cpu_offload","scalability"]}
{"claim_id":"clm_opt_matrix_scalable_second_order_2","claim_type":"causal","extracted_at":"2026-07-19","fingerprint":"5a4648661dd3bafa","limitations":["very long staleness eventually degrades the preconditioner","optimal period is task-dependent"],"locator":"method","paper_id":"pap_opt_matrix_scalable_second_order","scope":{"algorithm":"distributed_shampoo","knob":"preconditioner_update_period"},"statement":"Recomputing the preconditioner root only once every few hundred steps (stale preconditioning) retains most of the convergence benefit while amortizing its cost, so update frequency can be lowered with little loss.","tags":["preconditioner_staleness","amortization","update_frequency"]}
{"claim_id":"clm_opt_matrix_scalable_second_order_3","claim_type":"predictive","extracted_at":"2026-07-19","fingerprint":"9e098b7f466729f0","limitations":["preprint, not peer-reviewed","gains depend on tuning and layer sizes"],"locator":"results","paper_id":"pap_opt_matrix_scalable_second_order","scope":{"model":"transformer","task":"machine_translation"},"statement":"On a Transformer machine-translation model, distributed Shampoo reaches target validation quality in fewer steps and less wall-clock time than a tuned diagonal adaptive method (Adam/AdaGrad).","tags":["transformer","convergence_speed","wall_clock"]}
{"claim_id":"clm_opt_matrix_kfac_1","claim_type":"method_definition","extracted_at":"2026-07-19","fingerprint":"05bf86654124aeed","limitations":["block-diagonal (ignores cross-layer curvature)","Kronecker factorization is an approximation, exact only under independence assumptions"],"locator":"method","paper_id":"pap_opt_matrix_kfac","scope":{"algorithm":"kfac","curvature":"fisher_information","layer_type":"fully_connected"},"statement":"K-FAC approximates each layer's Fisher-information block as the Kronecker product of two smaller matrices (input activation covariance and output-gradient covariance), reducing curvature inversion from cubic to roughly linear in layer width.","tags":["kfac","natural_gradient","kronecker_factorization","fisher"]}
{"claim_id":"clm_opt_matrix_kfac_2","claim_type":"predictive","extracted_at":"2026-07-19","fingerprint":"79ec55f308c016d1","limitations":["per-step overhead of maintaining and inverting factors","results predate transformer LMs"],"locator":"results","paper_id":"pap_opt_matrix_kfac","scope":{"algorithm":"kfac","baseline":"sgd_momentum"},"statement":"K-FAC updates make far more progress per iteration than SGD-with-momentum, so it can optimize the training objective much faster while costing only several times more per step.","tags":["convergence_speed","per_step_cost","second_order"]}
{"claim_id":"clm_opt_matrix_kfac_3","claim_type":"mechanistic","extracted_at":"2026-07-19","fingerprint":"11f539b35559c54d","limitations":["invariance is approximate under the Kronecker/block-diagonal approximation"],"locator":"method","paper_id":"pap_opt_matrix_kfac","scope":{"algorithm":"kfac","property":"reparameterization_invariance"},"statement":"Rescaling the gradient by the approximate inverse Fisher makes the update approximately invariant to reparameterization and better conditioned than the raw gradient.","tags":["natural_gradient","conditioning","invariance"]}
{"claim_id":"clm_opt_matrix_kfac_modern_architectures_1","claim_type":"method_definition","extracted_at":"2026-07-19","fingerprint":"56b85cb48c0e9413","limitations":["exactness only holds for deep linear networks","no general theory for nonlinear weight-sharing layers"],"locator":"method","paper_id":"pap_opt_matrix_kfac_modern_architectures","scope":{"algorithm":"kfac_expand_reduce","architecture":"transformer_gnn_weight_sharing"},"statement":"For linear weight-sharing layers (as in transformers and GNNs) there are two distinct K-FAC variants, K-FAC-expand and K-FAC-reduce, that differ in whether the shared/sequence dimension is expanded or summed, and each is exact for deep linear networks in its respective setting.","tags":["kfac","weight_sharing","transformer","attention"]}
{"claim_id":"clm_opt_matrix_kfac_modern_architectures_2","claim_type":"descriptive","extracted_at":"2026-07-19","fingerprint":"fdbe5885cf5e72b0","limitations":["relative speed can depend on sequence length and layer shape"],"locator":"results","paper_id":"pap_opt_matrix_kfac_modern_architectures","scope":{"comparison":"reduce_vs_expand","cost_axis":"wall_clock"},"statement":"K-FAC-reduce is generally faster than K-FAC-expand because summing over the weight-sharing dimension gives cheaper Kronecker factors.","tags":["computational_cost","wall_clock","kfac"]}
{"claim_id":"clm_opt_matrix_kfac_modern_architectures_3","claim_type":"predictive","extracted_at":"2026-07-19","fingerprint":"d82687bade84826f","limitations":["depends on architecture and tuning budget","measured on graph/transformer/vision benchmarks, not billion-parameter LMs"],"locator":"results","paper_id":"pap_opt_matrix_kfac_modern_architectures","scope":{"baseline":"first_order","metric":"steps_to_target"},"statement":"Both K-FAC variants reach a fixed validation target in about 50-75% of the steps of a tuned first-order baseline, with a comparable reduction in wall-clock time.","tags":["convergence_speed","second_order","transformer"]}
{"claim_id":"clm_opt_matrix_sophia_1","claim_type":"method_definition","extracted_at":"2026-07-19","fingerprint":"6bdd7c11eae6ebf4","limitations":["diagonal Hessian ignores off-diagonal curvature","Hessian is estimated infrequently and stochastically"],"locator":"method","paper_id":"pap_opt_matrix_sophia","scope":{"algorithm":"sophia","preconditioner":"diagonal_hessian","task":"lm_pretraining"},"statement":"Sophia preconditions the EMA of gradients by the EMA of a light-weight diagonal Hessian estimate, updates the Hessian estimate only every k iterations, and clips the resulting update element-wise.","tags":["sophia","diagonal_hessian","clipping","language_model"]}
{"claim_id":"clm_opt_matrix_sophia_2","claim_type":"mechanistic","extracted_at":"2026-07-19","fingerprint":"303c8677186a73ff","limitations":["introduces a clipping threshold hyperparameter"],"locator":"method","paper_id":"pap_opt_matrix_sophia","scope":{"algorithm":"sophia","mechanism":"per_coordinate_clipping"},"statement":"Element-wise clipping bounds the update magnitude in coordinates with small or negative estimated curvature, preventing the blow-ups that would otherwise result from dividing by a tiny/negative Hessian estimate.","tags":["stability","curvature","clipping"]}
