{"claim_id":"clm_init_residual_xavier_glorot_init_1","claim_type":"method_definition","extracted_at":"2026-07-19","fingerprint":"6b2271bb04395b86","limitations":["Derived assuming linear or symmetric activations; not tuned for ReLU."],"locator":"method","paper_id":"pap_init_residual_xavier_glorot_init","scope":{"activation":"tanh_sigmoid_linear","regime":"initialization"},"statement":"Normalized (Xavier/Glorot) initialization sets each weight's variance to 2/(fan_in+fan_out) so that the variance of forward activations and of back-propagated gradients is balanced across layers.","tags":["xavier_init","variance_preservation","gradient_flow"]}
{"claim_id":"clm_init_residual_xavier_glorot_init_2","claim_type":"causal","extracted_at":"2026-07-19","fingerprint":"20002de581307f09","limitations":["Studied on pre-ReLU feedforward nets, not transformers."],"locator":"results","paper_id":"pap_init_residual_xavier_glorot_init","scope":{"depth":"deep_feedforward"},"statement":"Standard 1/sqrt(fan_in) initialization makes back-propagated gradient variance shrink as depth increases, which slows or stalls convergence of deep feedforward networks.","tags":["gradient_vanishing","depth","initialization"]}
{"claim_id":"clm_init_residual_xavier_glorot_init_3","claim_type":"mechanistic","extracted_at":"2026-07-19","fingerprint":"53627ff98578239c","limitations":["Mean-field/independence assumptions on inputs and weights."],"locator":"method","paper_id":"pap_init_residual_xavier_glorot_init","scope":{"regime":"initialization"},"statement":"Keeping layer-to-layer activation variance and gradient variance close to constant is what prevents saturation and vanishing signal in deep nets, motivating variance-preserving init.","tags":["signal_propagation","variance_preservation","saturation"]}
{"claim_id":"clm_init_residual_he_kaiming_init_1","claim_type":"mechanistic","extracted_at":"2026-07-19","fingerprint":"3ca092f4587463f8","limitations":["Assumes independent, zero-mean inputs and weights."],"locator":"method","paper_id":"pap_init_residual_he_kaiming_init","scope":{"activation":"relu_prelu"},"statement":"For ReLU/PReLU networks, initializing weights with variance 2/fan_in compensates for the rectifier halving the signal variance and preserves activation variance across layers, whereas Xavier init leaves it decaying.","tags":["he_init","relu","variance_preservation"]}
