{"claim_id":"clm_stability_spike_no_more_1","claim_type":"mechanistic","extracted_at":"2026-07-19","fingerprint":"d0b19b71abb1e7ef","limitations":["assumes gradient-norm growth as the proximate cause of spikes"],"locator":"method","paper_id":"pap_stability_spike_no_more","scope":{"analysis":"Jacobian spectral norm"},"statement":"Loss spikes are attributed to sudden growth of the gradient norm, which an analysis of the spectral norms of sub-layer Jacobian matrices links to large sub-layer amplification.","tags":["loss_spikes","gradient_norm","spectral_norm","jacobian"]}
{"claim_id":"clm_stability_spike_no_more_2","claim_type":"causal","extracted_at":"2026-07-19","fingerprint":"07a5e34192e6f98b","limitations":["validated at moderate scale relative to frontier models"],"locator":"results","paper_id":"pap_stability_spike_no_more","scope":{"conditions":"small sub-layers, large shortcut"},"statement":"Keeping sub-layer outputs small while keeping the residual shortcut large, achieved via a scaled initialization plus a simple modification to the embeddings, bounds the gradient norm and empirically prevents loss spikes.","tags":["initialization","embedding_scaling","residual","spike_prevention"]}
{"claim_id":"clm_stability_spike_no_more_3","claim_type":"descriptive","extracted_at":"2026-07-19","fingerprint":"106f0e881f453d3c","limitations":["gains depend on the specific architecture tested"],"locator":"results","paper_id":"pap_stability_spike_no_more","scope":{"comparison":"vs standard init"},"statement":"The proposed initialization plus embedding modification permitted stable pre-training at larger learning rates where standard baselines developed spikes or diverged.","tags":["learning_rate","initialization","stability"]}
{"claim_id":"clm_stability_small_scale_proxies_1","claim_type":"descriptive","extracted_at":"2026-07-19","fingerprint":"fa7157c0d6549770","limitations":["proxy validated on a limited set of instability types"],"locator":"abstract","paper_id":"pap_stability_small_scale_proxies","scope":{"proxy":"small models at high learning rate"},"statement":"Instabilities reported only at large scale, namely growth of attention logits and divergence of output logits from the log probabilities, can be reproduced in small Transformers by training at high learning rates, giving a cheap proxy for studying stability.","tags":["attention_logits","output_logits","learning_rate","proxy"]}
{"claim_id":"clm_stability_small_scale_proxies_2","claim_type":"causal","extracted_at":"2026-07-19","fingerprint":"a35e697c984a62ab","limitations":["effectiveness measured via learning-rate sensitivity, not full-scale runs"],"locator":"results","paper_id":"pap_stability_small_scale_proxies","scope":{"mitigations":"qk_norm, z_loss"},"statement":"Mitigations from large-scale runs remain effective in the small-model high-learning-rate regime: qk-layernorm curbs attention-logit growth and a z-loss curbs output-logit divergence, both widening the range of learning rates over which training is stable.","tags":["qk_norm","z_loss","learning_rate_sensitivity","mitigation"]}
{"claim_id":"clm_stability_small_scale_proxies_3","claim_type":"predictive","extracted_at":"2026-07-19","fingerprint":"7125f731161a12b8","limitations":["extrapolation validated over a bounded scale range"],"locator":"results","paper_id":"pap_stability_small_scale_proxies","scope":{"method":"learning-rate sensitivity scaling"},"statement":"Measuring how the loss responds to learning rate at small scale predicts the onset of instabilities, and interventions such as warmup, muP, and depth scaling shift the stable learning-rate range in ways that extrapolate across scale.","tags":["learning_rate","mu_p","warmup","scaling_prediction"]}
{"claim_id":"clm_stability_mixed_precision_training_1","claim_type":"method_definition","extracted_at":"2026-07-19","fingerprint":"0becd17596631e80","limitations":["requires per-model or dynamic selection of the loss scale"],"locator":"abstract","paper_id":"pap_stability_mixed_precision_training","scope":{"master_weights":"fp32","precision":"fp16"},"statement":"Training in FP16 while keeping an FP32 master copy of weights, scaling the loss to shift gradients into FP16's representable range, and accumulating FP16 products into FP32 matches FP32 accuracy across a wide range of architectures without hyperparameter changes.","tags":["fp16","loss_scaling","fp32_master_weights","accumulation"]}
{"claim_id":"clm_stability_mixed_precision_training_2","claim_type":"mechanistic","extracted_at":"2026-07-19","fingerprint":"458dadf35efa68a6","limitations":["scale factor too large causes overflow to Inf/NaN"],"locator":"method","paper_id":"pap_stability_mixed_precision_training","scope":{"precision":"fp16"},"statement":"Small-magnitude activation gradients underflow to zero in FP16's narrow dynamic range; multiplying the loss by a scale factor shifts these gradients up into the representable range and preserves them, then unscaling restores correct magnitudes before the weight update.","tags":["underflow","loss_scaling","dynamic_range","gradients"]}
{"claim_id":"clm_stability_mixed_precision_training_3","claim_type":"descriptive","extracted_at":"2026-07-19","fingerprint":"a390a5ca2ef31616","limitations":["speedups depend on FP16-capable hardware"],"locator":"results","paper_id":"pap_stability_mixed_precision_training","scope":{"benefit":"memory and throughput"},"statement":"The mixed-precision recipe roughly halves memory footprint and accelerates arithmetic on supporting hardware while matching full-precision accuracy on CNNs, RNNs, and language and translation models.","tags":["fp16","memory","throughput","accuracy_parity"]}
{"claim_id":"clm_stability_fp8_formats_1","claim_type":"method_definition","extracted_at":"2026-07-19","fingerprint":"24f93e8004abf671","limitations":["format defined for interchange, not a training recipe by itself"],"locator":"abstract","paper_id":"pap_stability_fp8_formats","scope":{"formats":"E4M3, E5M2"},"statement":"The paper specifies two FP8 encodings: E4M3 (4 exponent, 3 mantissa bits) for weights and activations, and E5M2 (5 exponent, 2 mantissa bits) for gradients; E4M3 gives up representing infinities and reserves only one NaN pattern in order to extend its usable dynamic range.","tags":["fp8","e4m3","e5m2","format_design"]}
{"claim_id":"clm_stability_fp8_formats_2","claim_type":"descriptive","extracted_at":"2026-07-19","fingerprint":"99573cac65563295","limitations":["evaluated with per-tensor scaling and select higher-precision paths"],"locator":"results","paper_id":"pap_stability_fp8_formats","scope":{"max_scale":"175B parameters"},"statement":"FP8 training matches 16-bit training result quality across CNNs, RNNs, and Transformer-based models, including language models with up to 175B parameters, using the same model and hyperparameters as the 16-bit runs.","tags":["fp8","accuracy_parity","transformers","large_language_models"]}
{"claim_id":"clm_stability_fp8_formats_3","claim_type":"descriptive","extracted_at":"2026-07-19","fingerprint":"ce513aab5ba7c511","limitations":["role-based split is a design guideline, not proven optimal"],"locator":"method","paper_id":"pap_stability_fp8_formats","scope":{"assignment":"per-tensor format"},"statement":"Gradients require the wider dynamic range of E5M2 while weights and activations tolerate the higher precision of E4M3, motivating assigning FP8 formats per tensor role rather than using a single FP8 format everywhere.","tags":["fp8","gradients","range_vs_precision"]}
{"claim_id":"clm_stability_fp8_lm_1","claim_type":"method_definition","extracted_at":"2026-07-19","fingerprint":"244cb3efe8b6055c","limitations":["some sensitive tensors are kept in higher precision"],"locator":"abstract","paper_id":"pap_stability_fp8_lm","scope":{"components":"gradients, optimizer states, collective communication"},"statement":"FP8-LM incrementally moves gradients, Adam optimizer moments, and distributed-parallel communication to FP8 with automatic per-tensor scaling, reporting no loss of accuracy and no hyperparameter changes relative to BF16.","tags":["fp8","optimizer_states","gradient_communication","auto_scaling"]}
{"claim_id":"clm_stability_fp8_lm_2","claim_type":"descriptive","extracted_at":"2026-07-19","fingerprint":"064db1109959548a","limitations":["speedup figures are hardware- and implementation-specific"],"locator":"results","paper_id":"pap_stability_fp8_lm","scope":{"hardware":"H100","model":"GPT-175B"},"statement":"For GPT-175B on an H100 platform, the FP8 mixed-precision framework reduced real memory usage by about 39% and trained about 75% faster than the BF16 Megatron-LM baseline.","tags":["fp8","memory","throughput","bf16_comparison"]}
{"claim_id":"clm_stability_fp8_lm_3","claim_type":"mechanistic","extracted_at":"2026-07-19","fingerprint":"c0e1d36942bf9657","limitations":["which tensors need higher precision is determined empirically"],"locator":"method","paper_id":"pap_stability_fp8_lm","scope":{"failure_mode":"underflow/overflow"},"statement":"Naively casting all tensors to FP8 induces underflow and overflow that destabilize training, so component-wise scaling and selective retention of sensitive tensors at higher precision are needed for stable FP8 LLM training.","tags":["fp8","underflow","overflow","mixed_precision"]}
{"claim_id":"clm_stability_fp8_trillion_token_1","claim_type":"mechanistic","extracted_at":"2026-07-19","fingerprint":"f5b654a951e9ce4c","limitations":["mechanism identified for SwiGLU-based FFNs specifically"],"locator":"results","paper_id":"pap_stability_fp8_trillion_token","scope":{"horizon":"late training","trigger":"SwiGLU outlier amplification"},"statement":"FP8 training instabilities invisible in short runs emerge only after prolonged training (hundreds of billions of tokens), caused by outlier amplification in the SwiGLU activation as its weights gradually align over the course of training.","tags":["fp8","swiglu","outliers","late_training_instability"]}
{"claim_id":"clm_stability_fp8_trillion_token_2","claim_type":"method_definition","extracted_at":"2026-07-19","fingerprint":"0bf943413409826e","limitations":["adds scaling bookkeeping around the activation"],"locator":"method","paper_id":"pap_stability_fp8_trillion_token","scope":{"granularity":"per-channel","method":"Smooth-SwiGLU"},"statement":"Smooth-SwiGLU applies per-channel scaling around the SwiGLU activation to keep quantized values in range, enabling stable FP8 training without changing the function's mathematical output.","tags":["fp8","activation_scaling","smooth_swiglu","outlier_control"]}
{"claim_id":"clm_stability_fp8_trillion_token_3","claim_type":"descriptive","extracted_at":"2026-07-19","fingerprint":"c70e7230d3a4e5c2","limitations":["demonstrated at 7B scale on one accelerator family"],"locator":"results","paper_id":"pap_stability_fp8_trillion_token","scope":{"hardware":"Gaudi2","model":"7B","tokens":"2T"},"statement":"A 7B model trained in FP8 on up to 2 trillion tokens matched the BF16 baseline while improving throughput by up to about 34%, and the Adam optimizer moments were also quantized to FP8.","tags":["fp8","throughput","optimizer_quantization","trillion_token"]}
{"claim_id":"clm_stability_deepseek_v3_fp8_1","claim_type":"method_definition","extracted_at":"2026-07-19","fingerprint":"3cb58813e7410844","limitations":["recipe is co-designed with specific hardware constraints"],"locator":"method","paper_id":"pap_stability_deepseek_v3_fp8","scope":{"granularity":"tile-wise and block-wise","model":"671B MoE","tokens":"14.8T"},"statement":"DeepSeek-V3 validates FP8 mixed-precision training at 671B-parameter, 14.8T-token scale using fine-grained quantization: tile-wise (1x128) scaling for activations and block-wise (128x128) scaling for weights, which localizes scaling factors to contain outliers.","tags":["fp8","fine_grained_quantization","tile_wise","block_wise"]}
{"claim_id":"clm_stability_deepseek_v3_fp8_2","claim_type":"mechanistic","extracted_at":"2026-07-19","fingerprint":"344b86d8920d7f3d","limitations":["accumulation-precision limit is specific to the tensor cores used"],"locator":"method","paper_id":"pap_stability_deepseek_v3_fp8","scope":{"fix":"FP32 accumulation promotion","hardware":"H800"},"statement":"Because the hardware's FP8 tensor-core accumulation retains only about 14 bits of precision, periodically promoting partial sums to FP32 on CUDA cores is required to prevent accumulation error from degrading FP8 training.","tags":["fp8","accumulation_precision","fp32_promotion"]}
{"claim_id":"clm_stability_deepseek_v3_fp8_3","claim_type":"descriptive","extracted_at":"2026-07-19","fingerprint":"cbf6e83dbc221fdb","limitations":["single production run; stability tricks not individually ablated"],"locator":"results","paper_id":"pap_stability_deepseek_v3_fp8","scope":{"model":"DeepSeek-V3","run":"full pretraining"},"statement":"Across the entire 14.8T-token pre-training run the training curve was stable, with no irrecoverable loss spikes and no rollbacks reported.","tags":["stability","loss_spikes","fp8","no_rollback"]}
{"claim_id":"clm_stability_gemma2_logit_softcap_1","claim_type":"method_definition","extracted_at":"2026-07-19","fingerprint":"e5e79721f10de045","limitations":["soft-capping is incompatible with some fused attention kernels and was later dropped in favor of qk-norm in successor models"],"locator":"method","paper_id":"pap_stability_gemma2_logit_softcap","scope":{"attention_cap":"50","final_cap":"30","function":"tanh"},"statement":"Gemma 2 soft-caps logits in every attention layer and at the final output layer using logit = cap * tanh(logit / cap), with an attention-logit cap of 50 and a final-logit cap of 30, bounding logit magnitude to prevent extreme values.","tags":["logit_soft_capping","attention_logits","output_logits","tanh"]}
{"claim_id":"clm_stability_gemma2_logit_softcap_2","claim_type":"causal","extracted_at":"2026-07-19","fingerprint":"4b213ac78edf102f","limitations":["presented as a design choice within a larger recipe rather than an isolated ablation"],"locator":"method","paper_id":"pap_stability_gemma2_logit_softcap","scope":{"mechanism":"tanh soft-cap"},"statement":"Bounding logits via soft-capping limits the extreme activations that could otherwise destabilize training, functioning as a guardrail on logit growth.","tags":["stability","logit_control","activation_bounding"]}
{"claim_id":"clm_stability_kimi_k2_qk_clip_1","claim_type":"mechanistic","extracted_at":"2026-07-19","fingerprint":"09636d6422b5c52f","limitations":["observed in the authors' large-scale MoE setting"],"locator":"method","paper_id":"pap_stability_kimi_k2_qk_clip","scope":{"optimizer_context":"Muon","threshold":"max logit > ~1000"},"statement":"Unchecked attention-logit growth, with maximum logits exceeding magnitudes of about 1000, is a source of training instability that produces loss spikes and occasional divergence, and this explosion is already evident when training with vanilla Muon at scale.","tags":["attention_logits","loss_spikes","divergence","logit_explosion"]}
{"claim_id":"clm_stability_kimi_k2_qk_clip_2","claim_type":"method_definition","extracted_at":"2026-07-19","fingerprint":"896b1e6598374fb0","limitations":["uses the observed max logit as a guiding signal; threshold is a hyperparameter"],"locator":"method","paper_id":"pap_stability_kimi_k2_qk_clip","scope":{"granularity":"per-head","threshold":"tau ~= 100"},"statement":"QK-clip rescales the query and key projection weights after each optimizer step so that per-head maximum attention logits stay below a threshold (about 100), controlling logit scale at its source without altering the current step's forward or backward computation.","tags":["qk_clip","attention_logits","weight_clipping","per_head"]}
{"claim_id":"clm_stability_kimi_k2_qk_clip_3","claim_type":"descriptive","extracted_at":"2026-07-19","fingerprint":"65882417d6e9cd23","limitations":["single large training run; QK-clip entangled with the Muon optimizer choice"],"locator":"results","paper_id":"pap_stability_kimi_k2_qk_clip","scope":{"tokens":"15.5T"},"statement":"Using MuonClip (Muon plus QK-clip) Kimi K2 was pre-trained on 15.5T tokens with no observable loss spikes, and the clip becomes inactive over time as maximum logits naturally decay into a normal operating range.","tags":["qk_clip","spike_free","muon_clip","self_deactivating"]}
{"claim_id":"clm_stability_glm_130b_1","claim_type":"causal","extracted_at":"2026-07-19","fingerprint":"d5adff735124e044","limitations":["reported from a single large-scale run"],"locator":"method","paper_id":"pap_stability_glm_130b","scope":{"technique":"embedding gradient shrink"},"statement":"Embedding-gradient shrink, which scales down the gradient flowing through the embedding layer, significantly stabilized GLM-130B training by damping the early-layer gradient magnitudes that otherwise triggered divergence.","tags":["embedding_gradient","gradient_shrink","stability"]}
{"claim_id":"clm_stability_glm_130b_2","claim_type":"mechanistic","extracted_at":"2026-07-19","fingerprint":"014ceb6743d58580","limitations":["tied to the specific hardware and precision available"],"locator":"method","paper_id":"pap_stability_glm_130b","scope":{"locus":"attention softmax","precision":"fp16"},"statement":"Low-precision computation in the attention softmax is prone to overflow and numerical collapse; abnormally large attention scores were identified as a source of instability, making the precision choice in softmax and attention decisive for convergence.","tags":["attention_logits","softmax","fp16","overflow"]}
{"claim_id":"clm_stability_glm_130b_3","claim_type":"method_definition","extracted_at":"2026-07-19","fingerprint":"dab2351a1344cb59","limitations":["recipe chosen empirically for this architecture"],"locator":"method","paper_id":"pap_stability_glm_130b","scope":{"recipe":"DeepNorm plus embedding gradient shrink"},"statement":"GLM-130B combines DeepNorm (a Post-LN scaling scheme) with embedding-gradient shrink as its stability recipe, selected after smaller-scale trials, to avoid the frequent divergences seen with naive configurations.","tags":["deepnorm","post_ln","stability_recipe","embedding_gradient"]}
{"claim_id":"clm_stability_bfloat16_study_1","claim_type":"descriptive","extracted_at":"2026-07-19","fingerprint":"9a2553e04da71b69","limitations":["lower mantissa precision than FP16; accumulation still done in FP32"],"locator":"abstract","paper_id":"pap_stability_bfloat16_study","scope":{"precision":"bf16","range":"equal to fp32"},"statement":"BFLOAT16 keeps the same exponent width, and therefore the same dynamic range, as FP32 while truncating the mantissa, and training in BF16 matches FP32 accuracy across image, speech, language, generative, and recommendation models in the same number of iterations.","tags":["bf16","dynamic_range","accuracy_parity"]}
{"claim_id":"clm_stability_bfloat16_study_2","claim_type":"mechanistic","extracted_at":"2026-07-19","fingerprint":"57999f95b89c9c63","limitations":["trades mantissa precision for range relative to FP16"],"locator":"method","paper_id":"pap_stability_bfloat16_study","scope":{"contrast":"bf16 vs fp16"},"statement":"Because BF16 preserves FP32's dynamic range, it converges without loss scaling and without hyperparameter changes, unlike FP16 whose narrow range requires loss scaling to keep small gradients from underflowing to zero.","tags":["bf16","fp16","loss_scaling","underflow"]}
{"claim_id":"clm_stability_gradient_clipping_adaptivity_1","claim_type":"method_definition","extracted_at":"2026-07-19","fingerprint":"10b86d04203b4bfe","limitations":["a smoothness model, validated on a limited set of networks"],"locator":"abstract","paper_id":"pap_stability_gradient_clipping_adaptivity","scope":{"condition":"(L0,L1)-smoothness"},"statement":"The paper introduces (L0,L1)-smoothness, a relaxed condition in which the local gradient-Lipschitz constant grows linearly with the gradient norm, and shows this fits observed neural-network training dynamics better than a single global Lipschitz constant.","tags":["relaxed_smoothness","gradient_clipping","theory"]}
{"claim_id":"clm_stability_gradient_clipping_adaptivity_2","claim_type":"causal","extracted_at":"2026-07-19","fingerprint":"f196cde6c41f0ab7","limitations":["convergence-rate result, not a direct account of loss-spike prevention"],"locator":"results","paper_id":"pap_stability_gradient_clipping_adaptivity","scope":{"methods":"clipping and normalized GD"},"statement":"Under (L0,L1)-smoothness, gradient clipping and normalized gradient descent provably converge arbitrarily faster than fixed-step-size gradient descent, providing a theoretical justification for why adaptively scaling large gradients accelerates training.","tags":["gradient_clipping","convergence","adaptivity"]}
{"claim_id":"clm_stability_baichuan2_maxz_normhead_1","claim_type":"method_definition","extracted_at":"2026-07-19","fingerprint":"dea3db2e309ce4d1","limitations":["auxiliary loss weight is a tuned hyperparameter"],"locator":"method","paper_id":"pap_stability_baichuan2_maxz_normhead","scope":{"target":"max output logit","technique":"max-z loss"},"statement":"Baichuan 2 adds a max-z loss that penalizes the magnitude of the maximum output logit, normalizing the softmax logits to stabilize training and to make inference less sensitive to decoding hyperparameters such as temperature.","tags":["max_z_loss","z_loss","output_logits","logit_control"]}
{"claim_id":"clm_stability_baichuan2_maxz_normhead_2","claim_type":"causal","extracted_at":"2026-07-19","fingerprint":"23a0ed47a7ad68d8","limitations":["benefit reported for this model family's recipe"],"locator":"method","paper_id":"pap_stability_baichuan2_maxz_normhead","scope":{"technique":"NormHead"},"statement":"NormHead, which normalizes the output-head (word) embeddings before computing logits, improves training stability and downstream performance and mitigates uncontrolled norm growth of rare-token embeddings.","tags":["normhead","output_embeddings","embedding_norm","stability"]}
{"claim_id":"clm_dynamics_grokking_progress_measures_1","claim_type":"mechanistic","extracted_at":"2026-07-19","fingerprint":"57d01cf49df1f01a","limitations":["single algorithmic task","small model; may not transfer to larger nets"],"locator":"results","paper_id":"pap_dynamics_grokking_progress_measures","scope":{"model":"one-layer transformer","task":"modular addition"},"statement":"Small transformers trained to grok modular addition implement a specific interpretable algorithm that uses discrete Fourier transforms and trigonometric identities to convert addition into rotation/composition on a circle.","tags":["grokking","mechanistic_interpretability","fourier_features","modular_addition"]}
{"claim_id":"clm_dynamics_grokking_progress_measures_2","claim_type":"temporal_association","extracted_at":"2026-07-19","fingerprint":"db31c0da87b15e5d","limitations":["demonstrated on algorithmic task","phase boundaries are approximate"],"locator":"results","paper_id":"pap_dynamics_grokking_progress_measures","scope":{"phases":"memorization -> circuit_formation -> cleanup","task":"modular addition"},"statement":"Grokking unfolds in three phases over training time (memorization, then gradual circuit formation, then cleanup of memorizing components), so the generalizing circuit forms continuously before test accuracy jumps.","tags":["grokking","phase_transition","circuit_formation","training_dynamics"]}
{"claim_id":"clm_dynamics_grokking_progress_measures_3","claim_type":"method_definition","extracted_at":"2026-07-19","fingerprint":"aad8dbf564118e54","limitations":["measures are task-specific and require knowing the circuit","not a general-purpose predictor"],"locator":"method","paper_id":"pap_dynamics_grokking_progress_measures","scope":{"measures":"restricted_loss, excluded_loss","purpose":"quantify hidden progress"},"statement":"Continuous mechanistic progress measures (restricted loss and excluded loss) derived from the learned circuit reveal hidden, gradual progress that precedes the abrupt improvement in test accuracy.","tags":["progress_measures","grokking","interpretability","hidden_progress"]}
{"claim_id":"clm_dynamics_grokking_lazy_to_rich_1","claim_type":"mechanistic","extracted_at":"2026-07-19","fingerprint":"985b7c4c4bd9737c","limitations":["core theory shown in a simplified polynomial-regression model","not all grokking instances proven to follow this mechanism"],"locator":"abstract","paper_id":"pap_dynamics_grokking_lazy_to_rich","scope":{"example":"two-layer net on polynomial regression, MNIST, one-layer transformer, student-teacher","setting":"vanilla gradient descent"},"statement":"Grokking can arise from a transition from lazy (kernel/linear) training dynamics to rich feature-learning dynamics, producing delayed generalization even without any explicit regularization.","tags":["grokking","lazy_training","feature_learning","rich_regime"]}
{"claim_id":"clm_dynamics_grokking_lazy_to_rich_2","claim_type":"causal","extracted_at":"2026-07-19","fingerprint":"692f8e607d23104e","limitations":["controlled synthetic settings","interacts with learning rate and width"],"locator":"results","paper_id":"pap_dynamics_grokking_lazy_to_rich","scope":{"knob":"initialization scale","outcome":"grokking delay"},"statement":"Increasing the initialization/output scale pushes dynamics toward the lazy regime and lengthens the delay before generalization, so the initialization scale controls the onset of grokking.","tags":["grokking","initialization_scale","lazy_vs_rich","causal_control"]}
{"claim_id":"clm_dynamics_grokking_lazy_to_rich_3","claim_type":"descriptive","extracted_at":"2026-07-19","fingerprint":"df48315f48a178ba","limitations":["still small/medium scale","evidence is empirical for the transformer/MNIST cases"],"locator":"results","paper_id":"pap_dynamics_grokking_lazy_to_rich","scope":{"domains":"MNIST, transformers, student-teacher"},"statement":"The lazy-to-rich transition mechanism reproduces and controls grokking across MNIST, one-layer transformers, and student-teacher networks, generalizing beyond regularization-based accounts.","tags":["grokking","generality","feature_learning"]}
{"claim_id":"clm_dynamics_edge_of_stability_1","claim_type":"temporal_association","extracted_at":"2026-07-19","fingerprint":"c4aa29bf727d6e26","limitations":["primarily full-batch GD; SGD behaves related but differently","empirical across architectures"],"locator":"results","paper_id":"pap_dynamics_edge_of_stability","scope":{"optimizer":"full-batch gradient descent","quantity":"top Hessian eigenvalue"},"statement":"During full-batch gradient descent the maximum Hessian eigenvalue (sharpness) rises until it reaches roughly 2/(learning rate) and then hovers just above that value for the rest of training, the 'edge of stability'.","tags":["edge_of_stability","sharpness","hessian","learning_rate"]}
