{"claim_id":"clm_r2_arch_gated_attention_2","claim_type":"mechanistic","extracted_at":"2026-07-19","fingerprint":"ec93d3a703c824ec","limitations":["measured on their trained checkpoints"],"locator":"results","paper_id":"pap_r2_arch_gated_attention","scope":{"phenomenon":"attention sink"},"statement":"The gate introduces elementwise non-linearity and sparsity that eliminate the attention sink, cutting attention allocated to the first token from ~46.7% to ~4.8%.","tags":["attention_sink_free","sparsity","non_linearity"]}
{"claim_id":"clm_r2_arch_gated_attention_3","claim_type":"predictive","extracted_at":"2026-07-19","fingerprint":"c4e59dda2c50a907","limitations":["stability claims tied to their training recipe"],"locator":"results","paper_id":"pap_r2_arch_gated_attention","scope":{"benefit":"optimization stability"},"statement":"Gated attention improves training stability, tolerates larger learning rates, and yields better scaling and length extrapolation than ungated softmax attention.","tags":["training_stability","learning_rate","scaling"]}
{"claim_id":"clm_r2_arch_selective_attention_1","claim_type":"method_definition","extracted_at":"2026-07-19","fingerprint":"88a73a1bf4744041","limitations":["soft-mask reuses existing attention signal; no learned gate"],"locator":"abstract","paper_id":"pap_r2_arch_selective_attention","scope":{"params_added":0},"statement":"Selective attention is a parameter-free change in which tokens emit a soft mask that reduces future tokens' attention to elements deemed no longer needed, adding zero new parameters and negligible compute.","tags":["parameter_free","soft_mask","context_pruning"]}
{"claim_id":"clm_r2_arch_selective_attention_2","claim_type":"causal","extracted_at":"2026-07-19","fingerprint":"cf3566c0cc1a9141","limitations":["equivalence measured on C4 across a range of sizes; not all downstream tasks"],"locator":"results","paper_id":"pap_r2_arch_selective_attention","scope":{"data":"C4","objective":"language modeling"},"statement":"On C4 language modeling, transformers with selective attention match standard transformers that have roughly 2x more heads and parameters in their attention modules.","tags":["quality_per_parameter","language_modeling"]}
{"claim_id":"clm_r2_arch_selective_attention_3","claim_type":"descriptive","extracted_at":"2026-07-19","fingerprint":"7747c46da814699e","limitations":["memory savings are inference-time; training still full-context"],"locator":"results","paper_id":"pap_r2_arch_selective_attention","scope":{"context_lengths":"512-2048"},"statement":"Selective attention lets the context buffer be pruned at inference, cutting attention memory 16x/25x/47x at context lengths 512/1024/2048 with no quality loss.","tags":["kv_cache_reduction","inference_efficiency"]}
{"claim_id":"clm_r2_arch_softpick_1","claim_type":"method_definition","extracted_at":"2026-07-19","fingerprint":"f5abb547974c2638","limitations":["not sum-to-one changes the interpretation of attention weights"],"locator":"abstract","paper_id":"pap_r2_arch_softpick","scope":{"replaces":"softmax normalization"},"statement":"Softpick is a drop-in rectified replacement for softmax that removes the sum-to-one constraint using a ReLU-based numerator, allowing total attention mass below one and exactly zero weights.","tags":["softmax_replacement","rectified","attention_sink_free"]}
{"claim_id":"clm_r2_arch_softpick_2","claim_type":"causal","extracted_at":"2026-07-19","fingerprint":"ee95b9de0dd46f34","limitations":["demonstrated only up to 1.8B parameters"],"locator":"results","paper_id":"pap_r2_arch_softpick","scope":{"models":"340M and 1.8B"},"statement":"Softpick drives the attention-sink rate to 0% and sharply lowers hidden-state kurtosis (340 vs 33,510) while producing ~47% sparse attention maps at 340M and 1.8B scale.","tags":["attention_sink","massive_activations","sparsity"]}
{"claim_id":"clm_r2_arch_softpick_3","claim_type":"negative_result","extracted_at":"2026-07-19","fingerprint":"da070d39d8fd265d","limitations":["parity, not improvement, in the main-precision LM setting"],"locator":"results","paper_id":"pap_r2_arch_softpick","scope":{"metric":"standard benchmarks / perplexity"},"statement":"Softpick only matches (does not beat) softmax on standard benchmarks at full precision; its measured advantage is confined to low-bit quantization robustness rather than lower validation loss.","tags":["quality_neutral","quantization"]}
{"claim_id":"clm_r2_arch_stick_breaking_attention_1","claim_type":"method_definition","extracted_at":"2026-07-19","fingerprint":"2bcbadeb902c4194","limitations":["requires a numerically stable custom kernel"],"locator":"abstract","paper_id":"pap_r2_arch_stick_breaking_attention","scope":{"replaces":"softmax+RoPE"},"statement":"Stick-breaking attention replaces the softmax normalization with a sequential stick-breaking allocation: each earlier token takes a sigmoid-gated fraction of the remaining attention mass, yielding weights with a built-in recency bias and no need for positional embeddings.","tags":["stick_breaking","implicit_position","softmax_replacement"]}
{"claim_id":"clm_r2_arch_stick_breaking_attention_2","claim_type":"causal","extracted_at":"2026-07-19","fingerprint":"046bfa2801cf7cb7","limitations":["competitive, with gains concentrated in length generalization"],"locator":"results","paper_id":"pap_r2_arch_stick_breaking_attention","scope":{"baseline":"softmax + RoPE"},"statement":"As a drop-in replacement for softmax+RoPE, stick-breaking attention performs competitively on downstream tasks and improves length generalization.","tags":["length_generalization","drop_in"]}
{"claim_id":"clm_r2_arch_stick_breaking_attention_3","claim_type":"descriptive","extracted_at":"2026-07-19","fingerprint":"057d8f8300ebeb1c","limitations":["custom Triton kernel needed for efficiency"],"locator":"abstract","paper_id":"pap_r2_arch_stick_breaking_attention","scope":{"kernel":"FlashAttention-adapted"},"statement":"The authors provide a numerically stable formulation with a FlashAttention-style implementation, making the mechanism practical at scale.","tags":["efficient_kernel","numerical_stability"]}
{"claim_id":"clm_r2_arch_peri_ln_1","claim_type":"method_definition","extracted_at":"2026-07-19","fingerprint":"9d92a96d06a64cee","limitations":["adds a second normalization per sublayer"],"locator":"abstract","paper_id":"pap_r2_arch_peri_ln","scope":{"placement":"peripheral (pre+post sublayer)"},"statement":"Peri-LN places normalization peripherally around each sublayer, applying LayerNorm both before and after the module and additionally normalizing the input and final output embeddings, unlike Pre-LN or Post-LN.","tags":["normalization_placement","peri_ln"]}
{"claim_id":"clm_r2_arch_peri_ln_2","claim_type":"mechanistic","extracted_at":"2026-07-19","fingerprint":"e4f18cdd6e08f50c","limitations":["benefits emphasized at large scale"],"locator":"results","paper_id":"pap_r2_arch_peri_ln","scope":{"comparison":"Pre-LN vs Post-LN"},"statement":"Peri-LN produces more balanced activation-variance growth and steadier gradient propagation than Pre-LN or Post-LN, stabilizing large-scale training.","tags":["variance_control","gradient_stability"]}
{"claim_id":"clm_r2_arch_polycom_1","claim_type":"method_definition","extracted_at":"2026-07-19","fingerprint":"80b7eea659e4d421","limitations":["polynomial terms can raise numerical sensitivity"],"locator":"abstract","paper_id":"pap_r2_arch_polycom","scope":{"replaces":"GELU / SwiGLU"},"statement":"PolyCom is a family of polynomial composition activations (composing a learnable polynomial with the input, e.g. PolyReLU/PolyNorm) proposed as a drop-in replacement for GELU and SwiGLU.","tags":["activation","polynomial_composition"]}
{"claim_id":"clm_r2_arch_polycom_2","claim_type":"descriptive","extracted_at":"2026-07-19","fingerprint":"5f46c09f30d4e047","limitations":["theoretical bound, expressivity not directly loss"],"locator":"abstract","paper_id":"pap_r2_arch_polycom","scope":{"analysis":"Sobolev approximation"},"statement":"PolyCom networks attain the optimal approximation rate for smooth functions in Sobolev spaces, indicating higher expressivity per parameter than ReLU- or GELU-based activations.","tags":["expressivity","approximation_theory"]}
{"claim_id":"clm_r2_arch_polycom_3","claim_type":"causal","extracted_at":"2026-07-19","fingerprint":"8fc800d20fe6153a","limitations":["gains reported on their pretraining/eval setup"],"locator":"results","paper_id":"pap_r2_arch_polycom","scope":{"models":"dense and MoE LLMs"},"statement":"Swapping in PolyCom improves accuracy and speeds convergence for dense and MoE LLMs on downstream tasks without significant added compute.","tags":["convergence_speed","downstream_accuracy"]}
{"claim_id":"clm_r2_arch_samba_1","claim_type":"method_definition","extracted_at":"2026-07-19","fingerprint":"aa77732990dc4eb8","limitations":["exact attention restricted to a local window"],"locator":"abstract","paper_id":"pap_r2_arch_samba","scope":{"hybrid":"SSM + sliding-window attention"},"statement":"Samba is a simple layer-wise hybrid that stacks a selective state-space (Mamba) layer, an MLP, a sliding-window attention layer, and an MLP, combining a linear-time recurrent mixer with short-range exact attention.","tags":["hybrid","sliding_window_attention","state_space_model"]}
{"claim_id":"clm_r2_arch_samba_2","claim_type":"causal","extracted_at":"2026-07-19","fingerprint":"7e2b8f912d8f6218","limitations":["headline numbers at 3.8B, not nanoGPT scale"],"locator":"results","paper_id":"pap_r2_arch_samba","scope":{"largest_model":"3.8B","tokens":"3.2T"},"statement":"The recurrent-plus-local-attention hybrid outperforms pure attention and pure SSM baselines at matched scale; Samba-3.8B (3.2T tokens) beats Phi3-mini on MMLU, GSM8K, and HumanEval.","tags":["quality_per_compute","benchmarks"]}
{"claim_id":"clm_r2_arch_samba_3","claim_type":"descriptive","extracted_at":"2026-07-19","fingerprint":"7b79797d79e6dae4","limitations":["extrapolation quality varies by task"],"locator":"results","paper_id":"pap_r2_arch_samba","scope":{"extrapolation":"256K-1M","train_context":"4K"},"statement":"Samba runs in linear time, extrapolates from 4K training context to 256K with perfect passkey recall, and gives ~3.7x higher throughput than grouped-query-attention Transformers at 128K context.","tags":["length_extrapolation","throughput","linear_time"]}
{"claim_id":"clm_r2_survey_efficient_llms_1","claim_type":"method_definition","extracted_at":"2026-07-19","fingerprint":"b9c2ab1192708b1c","limitations":["fast-moving field; taxonomy is a 2023-2024 snapshot","largely qualitative organization rather than head-to-head benchmarking"],"locator":"survey","paper_id":"pap_r2_survey_efficient_llms","scope":{"coverage":"training+finetuning+inference+serving","models":"LLMs"},"statement":"Organizes efficient-LLM research into three interconnected families: model-centric (model compression via quantization/pruning/low-rank/distillation, efficient pre-training, parameter-efficient and memory-efficient fine-tuning, efficient inference such as speculative decoding and KV-cache optimization, and efficient architecture design including efficient attention, mixture-of-experts, and transformer-alternatives), data-centric (data selection and prompt engineering/compression), and framework-centric systems (DeepSpeed, Megatron, vLLM, TensorRT-LLM).","tags":["efficient_training","taxonomy","model_compression","survey"]}
{"claim_id":"clm_r2_survey_efficient_llms_2","claim_type":"descriptive","extracted_at":"2026-07-19","fingerprint":"584b3fa97f18d897","limitations":["interactions between techniques are not exhaustively quantified"],"locator":"survey","paper_id":"pap_r2_survey_efficient_llms","scope":{"technique":"composition of efficiency methods"},"statement":"Efficiency gains compound when orthogonal techniques are stacked (e.g., quantization combined with LoRA as in QLoRA, or compression combined with efficient architectures) rather than relying on any single intervention.","tags":["efficient_training","technique_stacking","survey"]}
{"claim_id":"clm_r2_survey_efficient_llms_3","claim_type":"descriptive","extracted_at":"2026-07-19","fingerprint":"83346a56aaaaa513","limitations":["system findings depend on hardware and framework generation"],"locator":"survey","paper_id":"pap_r2_survey_efficient_llms","scope":{"regime":"large-scale training and serving"},"statement":"For LLMs, efficiency methods increasingly favor the post-training setting to avoid resource-intensive retraining, and system-level optimization (ZeRO, FSDP, vLLM) becomes a non-negotiable bottleneck at scale because algorithmic improvements alone cannot overcome the memory and communication walls.","tags":["system_optimization","post_training","survey"]}
{"claim_id":"clm_r2_survey_data_efficient_training_1","claim_type":"method_definition","extracted_at":"2026-07-19","fingerprint":"5d7be12a19a2f8f2","limitations":["centered on post-training/fine-tuning rather than large-scale pretraining","recent preprint; taxonomy newly proposed"],"locator":"survey","paper_id":"pap_r2_survey_data_efficient_training","scope":{"models":"LLMs","stage":"post-training"},"statement":"Proposes a taxonomy of data-efficient LLM (post-)training methods with five branches: data selection, data quality enhancement, synthetic data generation, data distillation and compression, and self-evolving data ecosystems.","tags":["data_efficiency","taxonomy","synthetic_data","survey"]}
{"claim_id":"clm_r2_survey_data_efficient_training_2","claim_type":"descriptive","extracted_at":"2026-07-19","fingerprint":"f98144b7d1431199","limitations":["diminishing-returns evidence aggregated across heterogeneous setups"],"locator":"survey","paper_id":"pap_r2_survey_data_efficient_training","scope":{"constraint":"annotation cost and data-scale saturation"},"statement":"The survey concludes that the prevailing post-training paradigm faces high manual-annotation cost and diminishing marginal returns on data scale, so maximizing per-example data utility (selection and quality enhancement) rather than adding more data has become the key research frontier.","tags":["data_efficiency","diminishing_returns","survey"]}
{"claim_id":"clm_r2_survey_data_efficient_training_3","claim_type":"predictive","extracted_at":"2026-07-19","fingerprint":"ccf713cb3c7a442b","limitations":["risk of distribution collapse / self-reinforcing bias not fully resolved"],"locator":"survey","paper_id":"pap_r2_survey_data_efficient_training","scope":{"direction":"future data pipelines"},"statement":"Synthetic data generation together with self-evolving data ecosystems (models generating and curating their own training data) are identified as the emerging route to escape the human-annotation bottleneck for continued capability gains.","tags":["synthetic_data","self_evolving","survey"]}
{"claim_id":"clm_r2_survey_dl_optimizers_1","claim_type":"method_definition","extracted_at":"2026-07-19","fingerprint":"8b910b2ba0dbc97b","limitations":["theoretical framing; not LLM-specific empirical wall-clock benchmarks"],"locator":"survey","paper_id":"pap_r2_survey_dl_optimizers","scope":{"domain":"deep-learning optimization","lens":"convergence+generalization"},"statement":"Organizes optimization methods for deep learning into generic first-order and second-order gradient methods, optimizers that adapt to properties of the loss landscape and explicitly encourage well-generalizing minima, and distributed optimization methods (centralized and decentralized), each treated with both convex and non-convex convergence analysis.","tags":["optimizers","taxonomy","convergence","survey"]}
{"claim_id":"clm_r2_survey_dl_optimizers_2","claim_type":"descriptive","extracted_at":"2026-07-19","fingerprint":"4e8777e5be8f1855","limitations":["assessment of prior surveys is qualitative"],"locator":"survey","paper_id":"pap_r2_survey_dl_optimizers","scope":{"target":"optimizer-survey literature"},"statement":"The survey's cross-literature meta-conclusion is that most prior optimizer surveys merely summarize methodologies while overlooking the critical theoretical analyses (convergence and generalization) needed to explain why optimizers behave as they do, a gap this handbook is written to close.","tags":["optimizers","theory_gap","survey"]}
{"claim_id":"clm_r2_survey_dl_optimizers_3","claim_type":"mechanistic","extracted_at":"2026-07-19","fingerprint":"80f08524416df7cc","limitations":["flat-minima-to-generalization link is theoretically motivated but debated"],"locator":"survey","paper_id":"pap_r2_survey_dl_optimizers","scope":{"family":"loss-landscape-adaptive optimizers"},"statement":"Landscape-adaptive optimizers (e.g., sharpness/flatness-aware methods) are characterized as explicitly biasing training toward flat, well-generalizing minima, connecting optimization dynamics directly to generalization rather than only to training-loss descent.","tags":["optimizers","generalization","sharpness_aware","survey"]}
{"claim_id":"clm_r2_survey_data_selection_1","claim_type":"method_definition","extracted_at":"2026-07-19","fingerprint":"14d5c92006f950d2","limitations":["empirical validation is expensive and concentrated in a few organizations that rarely share findings"],"locator":"survey","paper_id":"pap_r2_survey_data_selection","scope":{"models":"language models","stage":"pretraining+finetuning+ICL"},"statement":"Unifies data selection as a utility function (mapping data points to a real-valued utility) plus a selection mechanism, and categorizes methods along dimensions of distribution matching vs. diversification, dataset-level vs. data-point-level alteration, binary filtering vs. reweighting/mixing output spaces, and training stage (pretraining, instruction-tuning, alignment, in-context learning).","tags":["data_selection","taxonomy","pretraining","survey"]}
{"claim_id":"clm_r2_survey_data_selection_2","claim_type":"descriptive","extracted_at":"2026-07-19","fingerprint":"81ec94d4c9ad11ff","limitations":["absence of controlled comparisons limits confidence in any single heuristic"],"locator":"survey","paper_id":"pap_r2_survey_data_selection","scope":{"stage":"pretraining data filtering"},"statement":"For pretraining, the survey concludes that composed heuristic filter pipelines (language identification, deduplication, length and repetition filters) remain the known best practice and have not changed significantly, yet no study systematically compares the many possible heuristics head-to-head.","tags":["data_selection","heuristic_filtering","survey"]}
{"claim_id":"clm_r2_survey_data_selection_3","claim_type":"descriptive","extracted_at":"2026-07-19","fingerprint":"9c37a2fb20179daa","limitations":["bias magnitude varies by reference corpus and language"],"locator":"survey","paper_id":"pap_r2_survey_data_selection","scope":{"concern":"fairness of quality filtering"},"statement":"Quality filtering against reference corpora can introduce demographic bias, preferentially selecting text associated with wealthier, higher-educated, and urban populations, so quality-driven selection carries a fairness cost alongside its efficiency benefit.","tags":["data_selection","bias","survey"]}
{"claim_id":"clm_r2_survey_data_selection_4","claim_type":"descriptive","extracted_at":"2026-07-19","fingerprint":"827579ffa415f586","limitations":["trade-off frontier shifts with hardware and classifier efficiency"],"locator":"survey","paper_id":"pap_r2_survey_data_selection","scope":{"constraint":"compute cost of utility functions"},"statement":"A speed-quality trade-off fundamentally constrains method choice: cheap utility functions (n-grams, fastText) scale to billions of documents while more accurate transformer-based classifiers do not, bounding which selection techniques are usable at pretraining scale.","tags":["data_selection","scalability","survey"]}
{"claim_id":"clm_r2_survey_model_compression_1","claim_type":"method_definition","extracted_at":"2026-07-19","fingerprint":"6ff5294d67ca8034","limitations":["snapshot of 2023-2024 methods","effectiveness often coupled to specific hardware support"],"locator":"survey","paper_id":"pap_r2_survey_model_compression","scope":{"goal":"post-training compression","models":"LLMs"},"statement":"Structures LLM compression into quantization (quantization-aware training vs. post-training quantization, further split into weight-only, weight-activation, and KV-cache quantization), pruning (unstructured, structured, and semi-structured N:M sparsity), knowledge distillation (white-box vs. black-box), and low-rank factorization.","tags":["model_compression","quantization","pruning","survey"]}
{"claim_id":"clm_r2_survey_model_compression_2","claim_type":"descriptive","extracted_at":"2026-07-19","fingerprint":"b25d361882896f0e","limitations":["exact bit-width tolerance is model- and task-dependent"],"locator":"survey","paper_id":"pap_r2_survey_model_compression","scope":{"technique":"quantization bit-width vs accuracy"},"statement":"The survey synthesizes that weight-only post-training quantization (e.g., GPTQ, AWQ) preserves accuracy down to roughly 3-4 bits with minimal perplexity loss, whereas weight-activation quantization (e.g., SmoothQuant) must stay at higher precision such as INT8 because activation outliers otherwise degrade performance.","tags":["quantization","accuracy_tradeoff","survey"]}
{"claim_id":"clm_r2_survey_model_compression_3","claim_type":"descriptive","extracted_at":"2026-07-19","fingerprint":"d0f12a18be91ff9d","limitations":["realized speedups require matching hardware/kernels"],"locator":"survey","paper_id":"pap_r2_survey_model_compression","scope":{"technique":"pruning sparsity vs deployability"},"statement":"Across pruning methods, unstructured and semi-structured approaches (SparseGPT, Wanda) can reach about 50% sparsity with little accuracy loss but depend on hardware support (e.g., 2:4 sparsity on NVIDIA Ampere), while structured pruning is more deployment-friendly but generally requires fine-tuning to recover accuracy.","tags":["pruning","sparsity","hardware_coupling","survey"]}
{"claim_id":"clm_r2_survey_knowledge_distillation_1","claim_type":"method_definition","extracted_at":"2026-07-19","fingerprint":"085fac612e1fc524","limitations":["feature/white-box elicitation inapplicable to closed proprietary teachers"],"locator":"survey","paper_id":"pap_r2_survey_knowledge_distillation","scope":{"models":"LLMs","teacher":"proprietary or open"},"statement":"Structures LLM knowledge distillation around three pillars: algorithm (knowledge elicitation via labeling, expansion, data curation, feature/logit extraction, feedback, and self-knowledge, plus distillation objectives such as supervised fine-tuning, divergence minimization, reinforcement learning, and ranking optimization), skill (instruction following, alignment, agentic tool-use/planning, NLP tasks, multimodality), and verticalization (law, medicine/healthcare, finance, science).","tags":["knowledge_distillation","taxonomy","survey"]}
{"claim_id":"clm_r2_survey_knowledge_distillation_2","claim_type":"descriptive","extracted_at":"2026-07-19","fingerprint":"7b2c781b1a68aef7","limitations":["quality of transferred skills is bounded by teacher coverage"],"locator":"survey","paper_id":"pap_r2_survey_knowledge_distillation","scope":{"mechanism":"data augmentation as distillation"},"statement":"The survey concludes that data augmentation (teacher-generated, skill- and domain-specific training data) is the operative mechanism of LLM-era distillation, shifting KD from mere output mimicry/architecture compression toward transferring abstract capabilities such as reasoning patterns, preference alignment, and value alignment.","tags":["knowledge_distillation","data_augmentation","survey"]}
{"claim_id":"clm_r2_survey_knowledge_distillation_3","claim_type":"descriptive","extracted_at":"2026-07-19","fingerprint":"da1e848dc54c2c75","limitations":["gains constrained by teacher access and terms-of-service/legal limits on distilling proprietary models"],"locator":"survey","paper_id":"pap_r2_survey_knowledge_distillation","scope":{"outcome":"open-vs-proprietary gap"},"statement":"Distillation from strong proprietary teachers is found to significantly narrow, and sometimes close, the gap between proprietary and open-source models, with small models trained on high-quality curated/distilled data approaching much larger proprietary counterparts.","tags":["knowledge_distillation","open_vs_proprietary","survey"]}
{"claim_id":"clm_r2_survey_peft_1","claim_type":"method_definition","extracted_at":"2026-07-19","fingerprint":"7e5c54756257ced0","limitations":["about adaptation, not pretraining","accuracy-efficiency trade-offs are task-dependent"],"locator":"survey","paper_id":"pap_r2_survey_peft","scope":{"models":"large models","task":"fine-tuning"},"statement":"Categorizes PEFT algorithms into four families: additive PEFT (adapters, soft prompts such as prefix-tuning/prompt-tuning/p-tuning-v2, and (IA)3), selective PEFT (fine-tuning a subset of existing parameters via masks, e.g., BitFit, with unstructured vs. structural masking), reparameterized PEFT (low-rank decomposition and LoRA family, e.g., LoRA, DoRA, VeRA, KronA, Compacter), and hybrid PEFT (UniPELT, MAM Adapter, NOAH, S4).","tags":["peft","lora","taxonomy","survey"]}
{"claim_id":"clm_r2_survey_peft_2","claim_type":"descriptive","extracted_at":"2026-07-19","fingerprint":"91eed31b20c05de7","limitations":["merge-back benefit assumes single-adapter deployment"],"locator":"survey","paper_id":"pap_r2_survey_peft","scope":{"comparison":"inference overhead across PEFT families"},"statement":"The survey synthesizes that reparameterized LoRA-family methods are the most widely recognized/adopted and can merge adapted weights back into the base model for no extra inference burden, in contrast to serial adapter-based approaches that add inference latency unless specially redesigned.","tags":["peft","lora","inference_efficiency","survey"]}
{"claim_id":"clm_r2_survey_peft_3","claim_type":"descriptive","extracted_at":"2026-07-19","fingerprint":"414ef1a707c65d82","limitations":["scale thresholds are approximate and evolve with method design"],"locator":"survey","paper_id":"pap_r2_survey_peft","scope":{"factor":"model scale dependence of PEFT"},"statement":"PEFT efficacy is found to be scale-dependent: soft-prompt methods become reliably effective primarily for large models (roughly >11B parameters), indicating that the best PEFT choice depends on backbone scale.","tags":["peft","scale_dependence","survey"]}
{"claim_id":"clm_r2_survey_small_language_models_1","claim_type":"method_definition","extracted_at":"2026-07-19","fingerprint":"23892e29c725039a","limitations":["reported architecture choices are mostly empirical without strict ablation validation","runtime numbers are device-specific"],"locator":"survey","paper_id":"pap_r2_survey_small_language_models","scope":{"arch":"decoder-only transformer","params":"100M-5B"},"statement":"Surveys 70 open-source decoder-only SLMs (100M-5B parameters) along three axes: architecture (attention type MHA/MQA/GQA/MLA, standard vs. gated FFN, activation, normalization, vocabulary size, parameter sharing), training datasets (e.g., The Pile, RefinedWeb, RedPajama, FineWeb-Edu, DCLM-baseline), and training algorithms (Maximal Update Parameterization, knowledge distillation, two-stage pretraining).","tags":["small_language_models","taxonomy","architecture","survey"]}
{"claim_id":"clm_r2_survey_small_language_models_2","claim_type":"descriptive","extracted_at":"2026-07-19","fingerprint":"6f41ff32fb5ebeae","limitations":["confounded by concurrent architecture and recipe changes"],"locator":"survey","paper_id":"pap_r2_survey_small_language_models","scope":{"factor":"data quality vs quantity","regime":">1T tokens"},"statement":"Across models the survey concludes data quality often outweighs training-token quantity: SLMs trained on model-filtered datasets (FineWeb-Edu, DCLM-baseline) show superior performance, an effect that is especially pronounced once training exceeds roughly 1T tokens.","tags":["small_language_models","data_quality","survey"]}
{"claim_id":"clm_r2_survey_small_language_models_3","claim_type":"descriptive","extracted_at":"2026-07-19","fingerprint":"85871d0ab7343cc7","limitations":["optimal over-training amount not established; deployment-motivated rather than loss-optimal"],"locator":"survey","paper_id":"pap_r2_survey_small_language_models","scope":{"strategy":"deliberate over-training for deployment"},"statement":"SLMs are deliberately over-trained far beyond the Chinchilla compute-optimal point (often >1.5T tokens, with smaller models sometimes trained on more tokens than larger ones), trading extra training compute for better inference/deployment efficiency.","tags":["small_language_models","over_training","chinchilla","survey"]}
{"claim_id":"clm_r2_survey_small_language_models_4","claim_type":"descriptive","extracted_at":"2026-07-19","fingerprint":"5b1f27261d13ac05","limitations":["benchmark-dependent gains; latency measured on specific devices"],"locator":"survey","paper_id":"pap_r2_survey_small_language_models","scope":{"metric":"capability trend and latency","period":"2022-2024"},"statement":"From 2022 to 2024 SLM capabilities improved faster than large baselines (roughly +10-13% on commonsense/problem-solving/math vs. ~+7.5% for LLaMA-7B), narrowing the SLM-LLM gap; meanwhile equal-parameter models differ greatly in latency (e.g., Qwen2-0.5B ~31.9% faster than Qwen1.5-0.5B), showing hardware-architecture co-design matters as much as parameter count.","tags":["small_language_models","capability_trend","latency","survey"]}
{"claim_id":"clm_r2_survey_test_time_scaling_1","claim_type":"method_definition","extracted_at":"2026-07-19","fingerprint":"2b149d2b5448c0f9","limitations":["fast-evolving area; taxonomy is a 2025 snapshot"],"locator":"survey","paper_id":"pap_r2_survey_test_time_scaling","scope":{"models":"LLMs and reasoning models","phase":"inference/test-time"},"statement":"Proposes a unified multidimensional taxonomy of test-time scaling along four core dimensions: what to scale (e.g., parallel vs. sequential vs. hybrid compute, chain-of-thought length), how to scale (supervised fine-tuning, reinforcement learning, search and verification such as MCTS, self-consistency, best-of-N), where to scale (task/application domains), and how well to scale (accuracy and efficiency assessment).","tags":["test_time_scaling","inference_scaling","taxonomy","survey"]}
{"claim_id":"clm_r2_survey_test_time_scaling_2","claim_type":"descriptive","extracted_at":"2026-07-19","fingerprint":"8ada1e16a95c28e6","limitations":["gains are task-dependent and can be weak on knowledge-intensive tasks"],"locator":"survey","paper_id":"pap_r2_survey_test_time_scaling","scope":{"outcome":"inference compute -> reasoning gains"},"statement":"The survey concludes that allocating additional inference-time compute reliably elicits problem-solving/reasoning gains, not only on specialized math and coding tasks but also on general open-ended QA, and that TTS rose to prominence precisely as returns from scaling pretraining data and parameters diminished.","tags":["test_time_scaling","reasoning","survey"]}
{"claim_id":"clm_r2_survey_test_time_scaling_3","claim_type":"predictive","extracted_at":"2026-07-19","fingerprint":"b7e774b0e2f6bc69","limitations":["small-model-beats-large results hold only in specific regimes"],"locator":"survey","paper_id":"pap_r2_survey_test_time_scaling","scope":{"direction":"compute-optimal TTS"},"statement":"Synthesizing developmental trajectories, the survey identifies compute-optimal test-time scaling (where smaller models plus more inference compute can rival larger models under matched budgets) as a major direction, while flagging open challenges in generalizing across tasks and attributing which techniques cause gains.","tags":["test_time_scaling","compute_optimal","survey"]}
{"claim_id":"clm_r2_survey_scaling_laws_1","claim_type":"descriptive","extracted_at":"2026-07-19","fingerprint":"07aab77bfa922aa8","limitations":["a meta-analysis; does not itself derive a single canonical law"],"locator":"survey","paper_id":"pap_r2_survey_scaling_laws","scope":{"n_papers":"50+","topic":"neural scaling laws"},"statement":"Surveys 50+ scaling-trend studies (45 of which quantify trends with a power law) and organizes them by the choices that vary across studies: the specific equation being fit, the training setup, and the optimization method, showing each of these factors can alter the fitted law and thus a study's conclusions.","tags":["scaling_laws","taxonomy","survey"]}
{"claim_id":"clm_r2_survey_scaling_laws_2","claim_type":"descriptive","extracted_at":"2026-07-19","fingerprint":"6722e7630dcf6be1","limitations":["attribution of discrepancies to methodology is partly inferential"],"locator":"survey","paper_id":"pap_r2_survey_scaling_laws","scope":{"quantity":"optimal token-to-parameter ratio"},"statement":"The survey documents that prior works reach conflicting conclusions on core quantities such as the optimal token-to-parameter (data-to-model) ratio, and argues these discrepancies stem largely from differences in fitting methodology and training details rather than genuine disagreement about the underlying phenomenon.","tags":["scaling_laws","compute_optimal","token_parameter_ratio","survey"]}
{"claim_id":"clm_r2_survey_scaling_laws_3","claim_type":"descriptive","extracted_at":"2026-07-19","fingerprint":"7e957fb7720059e2","limitations":["checklist adoption is a recommendation, not an empirical result"],"locator":"survey","paper_id":"pap_r2_survey_scaling_laws","scope":{"issue":"reproducibility of scaling studies"},"statement":"Most surveyed scaling-law papers under-report the details needed to reproduce their fits; the survey therefore proposes a concrete checklist of items authors should report to make scaling studies reproducible and comparable.","tags":["scaling_laws","reproducibility","survey"]}
{"claim_id":"clm_r2_survey_scientific_agents_1","claim_type":"method_definition","extracted_at":"2026-07-19","fingerprint":"78c2d3bcd9c56195","limitations":["nascent area; benchmarks are immature and heterogeneous"],"locator":"survey","paper_id":"pap_r2_survey_scientific_agents","scope":{"agents":"LLM-based","domain":"AI-for-science / autonomous research"},"statement":"Organizes the field of LLM-based scientific agents along five facets: architectures, design, benchmarks, applications, and ethical considerations, spanning a research pipeline of hypothesis generation, experiment design, data analysis, and simulation.","tags":["ai_research_agents","scientific_discovery","taxonomy","survey"]}
{"claim_id":"clm_r2_survey_scientific_agents_2","claim_type":"descriptive","extracted_at":"2026-07-19","fingerprint":"f02cbec410fa9943","limitations":["distinction is conceptual; boundary cases exist"],"locator":"survey","paper_id":"pap_r2_survey_scientific_agents","scope":{"distinction":"scientific vs general agents"},"statement":"The survey concludes that scientific agents differ fundamentally from general-purpose LLM agents by integrating domain-specific knowledge, specialized tool sets, and robust validation/reproducibility mechanisms, which is what lets them handle complex scientific data and ensure reproducible results.","tags":["ai_research_agents","tool_use","validation","survey"]}
{"claim_id":"clm_r2_survey_scientific_agents_3","claim_type":"predictive","extracted_at":"2026-07-19","fingerprint":"62b122827609a65c","limitations":["autonomy roadmap is forward-looking and partly speculative"],"locator":"survey","paper_id":"pap_r2_survey_scientific_agents","scope":{"direction":"rising agent autonomy"},"statement":"The survey frames the trajectory of the field as moving toward increasing autonomy (from assisting humans on narrow tasks to closed-loop hypothesis-to-experiment-to-analysis systems), while identifying validation reliability, reproducibility, and ethics as the principal bottlenecks to trustworthy autonomous discovery.","tags":["ai_research_agents","autonomy","closed_loop","survey"]}
{"claim_id": "clm_fa3_intra_kernel_scope", "paper_id": "pap_flash_attention_3", "statement": "FlashAttention-3 accelerates attention via warp-specialized asynchrony and FP8, raising intra-kernel GPU utilization within the attention op; its contribution is confined to the attention kernel and does not address the per-step CPU kernel-launch/dispatch overhead incurred across the rest of a training step.", "claim_type": "mechanistic", "scope": {"applies_to": "attention_kernel", "domain": "transformer_lm_training"}, "limitations": ["Scope statement: FA3 does not itself measure CPU launch overhead; the orthogonality is inferred from what the kernel does, not an ablation."], "locator": "method / kernel design", "extracted_at": "2026-07-25", "tags": ["attention", "flash_attention_3", "throughput", "systems_kernel"], "fingerprint": "5989a804157fdb20"}
{"claim_id": "clm_galore_memory_lever", "paper_id": "pap_recent_optim_galore", "statement": "GaLore cuts optimizer-state memory by projecting gradients to a low-rank subspace, showing that reducing the per-step gradient/optimizer memory footprint is a usable training lever; it does not address dense-gradient materialization for hash-embedding (n-gram VE) tables, which sparse row writes target directly.", "claim_type": "descriptive", "scope": {"applies_to": "optimizer_memory", "domain": "llm_pretraining"}, "limitations": ["GaLore's mechanism (low-rank projection of dense matmul grads) differs from sparse row-writes for gather-only embedding tables; the link is the shared premise that gradient-memory footprint is reducible, not the method."], "locator": "method / motivation", "extracted_at": "2026-07-25", "tags": ["optimizer", "memory_efficient", "galore"], "fingerprint": "78dd0b41a169e0ee"}
